   "subject": "Re: How to get probability of factor score?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Phil,thanks for your reply!<div>Suppose your have a IQ test,finally you will get a theta of whole test.Besides the theta, you also want to know your position in population.</div><div>Now,how to get the position based on model and your theta?</div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "2L7OmWrBBgAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-06-09T04:35:49Z",
   "subject": "Re: How to get probability of factor score?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">It depends on the shape of the population distribution. You have a few options here: either assume that the population is some form (like normal, see my previous reply), or you can try to estimate its shape somehow, perhaps by comparing to all the saved factors scores or by estimating the shape of this density directly (e.g., via empirical histogram or davidian curves). It depends on which approach you&#39;re willing to tolerate, and how much data you have to estimate these associated parameters/effects. <div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_attr\">On Sun, Jun 9, 2019 at 12:22 AM Dorothy Miller &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">778...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div></div>"
