   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi,Phil,</div><div>Actually I mean position in sample(not population),then all data are known.</div><div>Then, this question is:how to get  probability density estimate of theta based on  sample data?<br></div><div>As the example,how to calculate the position when theta is 2.295489(we know the answer should be 1)<br></div><div><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "qoqWlCDdBgAJ",
   "from": "Phil Chalmers",
   "author": "b330c2028fed415b",
   "date": "2019-06-09T13:03:37Z",
   "subject": "Re: How to get probability of factor score?",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">If it&#39;s just the sample information you care about then why not do something like the following?<div><br></div><div>library(mirt)<br>dat &lt;- key2binary(SAT12,<br>                  key = c(1,4,5,2,3,1,2,1,3,1,2,4,2,1,5,3,4,4,1,4,3,3,4,1,3,5,1,3,1,5,4,5))<br>mod &lt;- mirt(dat, 1)<br>fs &lt;- fscores(mod)<br><br>theta &lt;- 2.295489<br>mean(fs &lt; theta)</div><div>[1] 0.9883333<br></div><div><br></div><div>And no, you don&#39;t know that 2.295 should have a probability of 1. Even under the theoretical normal this is </div><div><br></div><div>pnorm(theta)<br></div><div>[1] 0.9891474<br><div><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\" class=\"gmail_signature\" data-smartmail=\"gmail_signature\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Phil</div></div></div></div><br></div></div></div><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div>"
    ],
